<main id="multibranch-pipelines-page" class="container py-5">

    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/Jenkins/Views/Shared/JenkinsTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- MAIN CONTENT -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <!-- PAGE TITLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h1 class="fw-bold">Multibranch Pipelines</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    Learn the concepts, branch discovery rules, repository scanning mechanisms, and dynamic staging patterns required to orchestrate automated scaling across multi-branch environments.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">What is a Multibranch Pipeline?</h2>
                <p>
                    In an active modern development team, software engineers continuously create short-lived feature branches, hotfixes, and pull requests. Manually setting up a unique Jenkins tracking item for each individual branch creates massive administrative debt. A **Multibranch Pipeline** resolves this bottleneck by turning your master controller into an autonomous search engine that auto-discovers and sets up execution tracks on its own.
                </p>

                <div class="alert alert-info my-3">
                    <strong>Simple Definition:</strong> A Multibranch Pipeline is a specialized Jenkins job type that scans a target Git repository, automatically creates individual nested pipeline tracks for every branch containing a `Jenkinsfile`, and automatically tears them down when those branches are deleted.
                </div>

                <h3 class="h4 fw-bold mt-4">Core Automation Mechanisms</h3>
                <p class="mb-2">Jenkins manages multi-branch orchestration using explicit platform capabilities:</p>
                <ul class="mb-3">
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>Repository Index Scanning:</strong> The discovery hook where Jenkins loops through your remote source control platform (GitHub, GitLab) to locate all active branch tracking paths.</li>
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>Dynamic Orphan Lifecycle Control:</strong> The automated housecleaning system that immediately purges or deactivates pipeline records inside the UI dashboard as soon as matching remote branch strings are merged and deleted.</li>
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>The `BRANCH_NAME` Variable:</strong> A built-in environmental macro populated automatically by the execution framework (e.g., matching `main`, `develop`, or `feature/login`), enabling branch-aware routing logic.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pull Request Evaluation:</strong> The native capability to parse inbound pull/merge requests, spinning up isolated validation runs ahead of merging source layers into stable tracks.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- KEY CONCEPTS -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Key Concepts</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Zero-UI Configuration Scaling</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Understanding that developers can launch entirely fresh end-to-end continuous integration pipelines without ever opening the Jenkins dashboard web menu, simply by pushing a text code asset inside a fresh branch.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Branch-Targeted Environment Routing</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Leveraging the structural `when` directive alongside branch keywords to selectively execute specific actions (like deploying to production servers) only when code commits run on specific branch tracks.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- EXAMPLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practical Jenkins Example</h2>
                <p>The following full declarative pipeline configuration showcases how a single `Jenkinsfile` routes step paths dynamically based on the current branch context populated by a Multibranch Pipeline structure:</p>
                <pre><code>pipeline {
    agent any
































    stages {
        stage('Initialize and Test Core') {
            steps {
                echo "INFO: Beginning validation pipeline pass on active branch track: \${env.BRANCH_NAME}"
                sh 'echo "Running standardized test suites across workspace targets..."'
            }
        }

        stage('Staging Deployment') {
            // Evaluates the dynamic environment parameter to only deploy staging branches
            when {
                branch 'develop'
            }
            steps {
                echo 'INFO: Branch matched [develop]. Transferring application deliverables to Staging nodes...'
                sh 'echo "Staging environment update finalized clean."'
            }
        }

        stage('Production Release Gate') {
            // Enforces strict safety boundary parameters to lock production delivery targets
            when {
                branch 'main'
            }
            steps {
                echo 'WARNING: Core branch matched [main]. Deploying validated build deliverables to Production servers...'
                sh 'echo "Production deployment processing complete."'
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
    </div>

<!-- PRACTICE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practice Exercise: Deploy an Automated Repository Scan Job</h2>
<div class="concept-box">
<h5 class="fw-bold">Step-by-Step Task Sequence:</h5>
<ol class="ps-3 mb-0">
<li class="mb-2">Open the main portal home screen dashboard layout and select the **New Item** sidebar menu link option.</li>
<li class="mb-2">Input the name string <code>Multi-Branch-App-Orchestration</code>, choose the card explicitly labeled **Multibranch Pipeline**, and click **OK**.</li>
<li class="mb-2">Locate the **Branch Sources** property selector block, add **Git**, and paste a target repository path URL containing multiple operational branches and a root `Jenkinsfile`.</li>
<li class="mb-2">Click the green **Save** button to write options down to disk storage. Jenkins immediately fires an initial automated repository scan pass.</li>
<li>Open the job landing dashboard view area once the scan finishes to see Jenkins dynamically present a neat list layout showing independent nested pipeline folders for each active branch.</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>

            <!-- SUMMARY -->
            <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
                <h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">You have completed the <strong>Multibranch Pipelines</strong> lesson. Continue through the syllabus to learn how to connect these multi-branch scanning nodes to automatic instant updates by exploring the Git Webhooks lesson.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- NAVIGATION -->
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
